    "instruction": "Assign Modify(subject, object) when subject syntactically modifies object, subject is the modifier (adjective, numeral, determiner, quantifier, clinical qualifier such as 'mild', 'focal', 'diffuse', 'acute', 'stable', 'right', 'upper', 'thoracic', etc.), and object is the head noun/entity being modified. The modifier usually directly precedes the head, but post-modifiers may also occur. Do not assign Modify when subject label is ANAT and object label starts with OBS; such pairs indicate a spatial/locative relation that should be captured by LocatedAt (with the OBS entity as subject and the ANAT entity as object) when a locative preposition or genitive construction is present, otherwise no relation.",
    "applies_when": "Two entities are adjacent or in a short modifying phrase, one narrows or describes the other, the modifier is the dependent and the head is the primary entity, and it is not the case that subject label is ANAT and object label begins with OBS.",
    "exceptions": [
      "Do not use Modify for spatial location relations (use LocatedAt) unless the word is an adjective describing a property of the head.",
      "Do not use Modify when subject label is ANAT and object label begins with OBS; use LocatedAt with reversed arguments if locative relation exists, otherwise no relation.",
      "Do not use Modify between independent concepts separated by conjunction."
    ],
    "priority": 1,
    "examples": []
  },
  "rationale": "The error evidence shows numerous spurious Modify edges where an anatomical entity (ANAT) is incorrectly linked as modifier to an observation/finding (OBS), e.g., pleural->effusion, pulmonary->nodule, mediastinal->lymphadenopathy, etc. In the gold annotation, such relationships are typically encoded as LocatedAt with the observation as subject and the anatomical location as object, not as Modify. The verified true positive contrasts contain no Modify edge with ANAT subject and OBS object, indicating that adding this restriction will reduce false positives without harming true positives."
}

